WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.800
A muscle lifts nothing.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.760
It shortens as fast as it can, and it does no external work.

3
00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.540
Another muscle holds a load it cannot move.

4
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.520
It also does no external work.

5
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.520
At both extremes, the external work is zero.

6
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.840
So where does a muscle deliver its power?

7
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:24.520
In 1938, A. V. Hill asked what sets how fast an activated muscle shortens.

8
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:30.193
Hill used isolated frog sartorius muscles at about 0 degrees Celsius.

9
00:00:30.873 --> 00:00:32.233
He tetanized each muscle.

10
00:00:32.873 --> 00:00:35.553
Then he let it shorten against a series of loads on a lever.

11
00:00:36.153 --> 00:00:37.653
A thermopile recorded the heat.

12
00:00:38.193 --> 00:00:41.853
He read the speed from the record of shortening, not from the total distance

13
00:00:41.853 --> 00:00:44.953
shortened. Heavier loads gave slower shortening.

14
00:00:45.917 --> 00:00:50.597
Speed fell with load along a curve called a rectangular hyperbola, not along a

15
00:00:50.597 --> 00:00:53.517
straight line. A viscous brake would give a straight line.

16
00:00:54.077 --> 00:00:57.217
So Hill's result ruled out a passive viscous-elastic muscle.

17
00:00:57.857 --> 00:01:01.517
Here is the curve for a made-up model muscle, not measured human values.

18
00:01:02.177 --> 00:01:04.717
Its isometric force is 100 newtons.

19
00:01:05.277 --> 00:01:08.757
With no load, it shortens at 0.40 metres per second.

20
00:01:09.297 --> 00:01:11.237
At 100 newtons, it does not shorten.

21
00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:14.787
Power is force times speed, in watts.

22
00:01:15.507 --> 00:01:19.587
With no load, the speed is greatest, but the load is zero, so the power is zero.

23
00:01:20.287 --> 00:01:24.327
When the load equals the force the muscle can hold, the speed is zero, so the power

24
00:01:24.327 --> 00:01:28.347
is zero again. This is external power, not ATP use.

25
00:01:28.847 --> 00:01:32.907
An isometric muscle does no external work, yet it keeps splitting ATP.

26
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.080
In between, power peaks.

27
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.300
In this model, the constant is one quarter of the isometric force.

28
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.460
Then the peak sits near 0.31 of the isometric force.

29
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.260
That is a load of 30.9 newtons.

30
00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.720
The speed there is about 0.12 metres per second, and the peak power is about 3.8

31
00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.780
watts. The exact fraction depends on the curvature.

32
00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.900
Now stretch the same model fibre.

33
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.980
Assume force falls along a straight line, from full force at 2.2 micrometres of

34
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:08.340
sarcomere length to zero at 3.65 micrometres.

35
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.280
At 3.00 micrometres, the isometric force is 44.83 newtons.

36
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.980
At fixed speed and curvature, peak power falls by the same factor, 0.448.

37
00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:23.900
The speed at peak power stays at 0.309 of top speed.

38
00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:30.100
Hill's rule gives no molecular mechanism, and its constants are frog values at about

39
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.360
0 degrees Celsius.

40
00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:36.060
External power is zero at both extremes and peaks at a middle load.
